    "slug": "ergonomic-typing",
    "content": "I've currently dug myself into a bit of a rabbit hole recently, that I'm still struggling to climb out of. And so I thought the best way to help me escape is by sharing all of the suffering I've caused myself over the past month with you, dear reader, by introducing you to the massively complex world of custom keyboards. Because as we'll see, there is so much more you can do with the humble little device you type on every day than you could ever imagine.\n\n<!-- make this introduction longer -->\n\n## QWERTY: The OG\n\nThe design of the QWERTY keyboard layout can be traced to Christopher Latham Sholes, who along with his colleagues, invented the machine known as the \"Type-Writer\", the mechanical version of the modern keyboard. Many legends are told about the origin of Sholes's seemingly arbitrary key placements. A common claim is that the key placement was designed so that frequent two letter combinations in English such as \"th\", \"he\", \"in\", and \"en\", would be far apart from each other on the keyboard. Some claim this was done to [[intentionally slow typists down](http://www.straightdope.com/columns/read/221/was-the-qwerty-keyboard-purposely-designed-to-slow-typists) (for what purpose I don't know) while others claim this was done to prevent keyjamming, which was a common problem with early typewriters where one key would get stuck on an adjacent one and prevent it from depressing[1](https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/the-qwerty-keyboard-will-never-die-where-did-the-150-year-old-design-come-from-49863249/). \n\nHowever, both of these theories have some major issues, due to the fact that \"er\" and \"de\" are very common in English, yet their corresponding keys are right next to each other. [This paper](https://repository.kulib.kyoto-u.ac.jp/server/api/core/bitstreams/dc434be9-80cd-499b-a984-f9fa35954c3b/content) tracks the evolution of the QWERTY keyboard and sheds light on the issues with these theories, as well as offering an alternative that explains other oddities of the layout. They claim that an early ancestor to the QWERTY layout took inspiration from an earlier attempt at a keyboard based on a piano, with the black keys containing the start of the alphabet in order, and the white keys containing the rest in reverse order. Sholes then took this layout and moved the vowels and Y to the top row, along with punctuation. This explains why most vowels are found in the top row, and the sequence of almost alphabetic letters in the home row DFGHJKL.\n\n\n## Why Change It?\n\n## Alt Layouts\n\n### Dvorak\n\n### Colemak\n\n### And so many more...\n\n## Keyboard Mods\n\n### Angle Mod\n\n### Curl Mod\n\n### Wide Mod\n\n### Home Row Mods\n\n### Symbol Layer\n\n<!-- add dreams of code videos here -->\n\n## Ergonomic Keyboards\n\n### Rowstag\n\n### Ortholinear\n\n### Columnstag\n\n### The 3rd Dimension\n\n<!-- dactyl glove80, etc -->\n\n### Special Mention: ARTSEY\n\n## Steno\n\n## How I Actually Type\n\n## My Custom Keyboard Layout\n\n### xmodmap\n\n### setxkbmap\n\n### keyd\n\n### Symbol Layer\n\n## What I've Learned\n\n\n<!-- sources: -->\n<!-- https://colemakmods.github.io/ergonomic-mods/wide.html -->\n<!-- https://bastardkb.com/product/skeletyl  -->\n<!-- https://colemakmods.github.io/mod-dh/keyboards.html -->\n<!-- Things to talk about\nworkshop the title\n\nkeyboard mods: angle mod, wide mod, thumb modifiers\nalternate keyboard layouts: dvorak, colemak, gallium, beakl, etc.\nergo keyboards: col stag, ortholinear, keywell, etc\nsteno\nartsey\n\nend with my weird keyboard posture and how it resolves many ergo issues with row-stag qwerty (differing finger lengths, hands bent at more comfortable angle, easy to reach alts for thumb modifiers)\n\nthen conclude with my symbol layer and my customized alt keys to go full circle back to the beginning\n\n\n\nextend layer ideas: arrow keys, tab, alt tab, enter, escape, home row mods???\n-->",
